 "caption": "This pile of skulls is a gift.\n\nVasily Vereshchagin painted The Apotheosis of War in 1871, and he had seen war up close. Three years earlier he was with the Russian army at the siege of Samarkand, in Central Asia, and was decorated with the Cross of St George for bravery.\n\nThen he painted what war leaves behind: a dead plain, a ruined city, crows, and a pyramid of skulls. Russia banned it from public exhibition, and in Germany Field Marshal Moltke reportedly kept his soldiers away from it.\n\nIn 1904 Vereshchagin went to war one last time, aboard the battleship Petropavlovsk. On 13 April she hit mines off Port Arthur and sank; he died with nearly 700 others, among them Admiral Makarov.\n\nHe had first called the painting The Triumph of Tamerlane. Then he wrote on the frame: \"Dedicated to all the great conquerors: past, present and future.\"\n\nIt hangs in the Tretyakov Gallery, in Moscow.\n\n👁️ Who would you dedicate it to?",
 "caption_short": "This pile of skulls is a gift, and the painter wrote on the frame who it's for. 👁️",
 "youtube_title": "Who is this gift for? Vereshchagin's The Apotheosis of War",
